Z||000000||Denise||05-23-2000||08:30 AM||||Never thought I'd get starry eyed over an engine!  I saw a couple of Fieros with 3800 SC's in them.  I WANT ONE!!! WAAAAAA!!!!  <P>Seriously, how do I start?  The sellers of the engine built and ready to go are all looking for $3500 - $4500.  Surely everything can be found and then built by me (yes me) and with some (allot) help from my local shop. I'm sure it's not feasable but I do love that engine! <P>Yes, BTW i'm iputing from 2 names DelValFieros@aol.com not a club,  and MSHVAC@aol.com<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Phil||05-23-2000||08:48 AM||||That's what it is going to cost even if you do it all yourself. The wiring of the computer is one of the biggies. Save yourself a few months of aggravation and have somebody that has the experience do it.<BR>Phil(SC3800 5sp)<BR>If you were at Carlisle mine was the yellow Formula<p>[This message has been edited by Phil (edited 05-23-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||redfiero||05-23-2000||11:30 AM||||Denise,<BR>I have been on the lookout for a 3800 SC myself.  I have found one for $2525 including shipping.  There is also a local salvage yard here in Cincinnati that wants me to call them every Friday and ask if they have any coming in.  The guy said that he will do a custom pull for me.  Most recyclers remove the alt., p/s pump and a/c from the engine.  Oh yeah...they almost never save the wiring harness and it costs $500 from GM.  Good Luck with the hunt!!||||reg||1
Z||000003||lowCG||05-23-2000||11:40 AM||||The Northwest isn't a great place to find "good'ol American"(more PC than other terms I've seen here lately)engines.When I first set out to find a different V6 for my car I lived in Tacoma,and found only one donor car at a junkyard with too many miles on it.After getting to know Michigan a little better,I found a GM dismantler with six,all fairly low mileage units.I got one('97,3.4,four camV6)for $1500 with 30K miles.<BR>You might try calling Proctor at Schram Auto,1.800.462.9292.<BR>It's a lot of work,but you'd be getting a lot of newer technology as well as a low mileage motor.Chris West has lots of special things for sale that help with the swaps also.<BR>Phil,can I ask you some ?s when I get my wires ready to patch together? I've got each wire identified from both harness',and was wondering if there are any special instructions in dealing with any of the late model ecm inputs(auto tranny inputs-do they need to be used? Anything else unusual?)<P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||BENGAL4||05-25-2000||03:28 PM||||Denise - My 3800 S/C swap is going to cost around $7,000.00 by the time everything is finished. I am having The Fiero Shop in Toronto do the honors. I think I could have done it but, my car would have been down for awhile. A long while.<P>redfiero - I am from Cincinnati also. I found my engine in a salvage yard in Clermont county. It's a 1997 Series II from a Buick. It only has 8,000 miles on it and I got it and everything I need including the ECM and harness for $1,800.00. Carthage Auto Parts found one in Toledo, but I came across this deal. Give them a call.||||reg||14
